cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AI for Renewable Energy Systems: Applied Research Portfolio"
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Murat
    family-names: Kus
abstract: >-
  Three engineering research projects on the German electricity system; three
  earlier ones are archived and still tested. Project 01 controls a prosumer PV
  and battery system every 15 minutes with rolling-horizon model predictive
  control (MPC) and a reinforcement-learning agent (SAC), on real German
  day-ahead prices, modelled weather (Open-Meteo) and household load measured
  in 2010 (HTW Berlin). For one household
  over 20.5 months out of sample, the MPC recovers 89.4 % of the cost headroom
  between a rule-based controller and perfect foresight, and the agent 32.4 %
  (median of three seeds). Project 02 dispatches a wind, PV and battery hybrid
  plant with a benchmark ladder that ends at rolling MPC; no learned controller
  is built. Project 03 forecasts a fleet-shaped wind and PV portfolio
  with a quantile forecast ladder from climatology to LightGBM, benchmarks it
  against the forecast the transmission system operators publish, and settles
  the positions at real day-ahead, intraday (ID AEP) and imbalance (reBAP)
  prices. In its 2026 test window LightGBM's day-ahead pinball loss is 2.41 MW,
  against 7.29 MW for climatology and 1.56 MW for the published forecast, and in
  neither of its two windows does any forecast's day-ahead gain in euros over
  climatology have a 90 % interval that excludes zero. Each project models the
  German market rules it depends on; Projects 01 and 03 tie their main results to
  committed CSV files.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - model predictive control
  - energy systems
  - electricity markets
  - renewable energy
  - probabilistic forecasting
  - quantile regression
  - imbalance settlement
  - German energy regulation
  - battery storage
